# THE INPAINTING PASS LOOKS AT 4 BY 4 PIXELS A THREAD, AND WORKS ONLY ON A PIXEL IT CAN CHANGE (2026-10-10, the own
# build's stage 3).
#
# WHAT AMD'S CODE DOES. The last pass runs one thread for every pixel of the display, 8.3 million at 3840x2160. A thread
# loads the generated colour and the inpainting weight and changes the pixel in at most three cases: the weight asks
# for inpainting, a HUD-less image is attached and differs there from the presented one, or a debug drawing is asked
# for. On a frame of the game few pixels ask; the pass costs 62 us all the same.
#
# WHAT THE PROBES SAID (stage 3, each with the pass's time): never inpainting 61 us; the weight's load alone and a store
# that never happens 60; the colour's load alone 60; an empty pass 6. So the 62 us are not the loads and not the
# inpainting: they are 8.3 million threads that each do next to nothing. And one thread for 4 by 4 pixels running AMD's
# whole function 16 times was slower, 111 us (the cut fi-inpainting-blocks, left out).
#
# THE CUT, FOR A DISPATCH WITH NO HUD-LESS IMAGE AND NO DEBUG DRAWING (the product's shape: the two-image set). One
# thread for 4 by 4 pixels, a sixteenth of the groups, and for each of its 16 pixels ONE question first: is the pixel's
# weight above AMD's threshold? Only then is AMD's function called for that pixel, unchanged, and it does there what it
# did. For every other pixel AMD's function loads two values and stores nothing: not calling it changes nothing. A
# pixel beyond what AMD's dispatch covered (the display's size rounded up to eight) is never touched, as before.
#   WITH A HUD-LESS IMAGE ATTACHED, OR A DEBUG DRAWING ASKED FOR, AMD's function may change any pixel, and the pass is
#   dispatched and runs as AMD's does: one thread a pixel, AMD's call, nothing else. (The first form of this cut used
#   the blocks there too, and the price said what that costs: every pixel worked on, 16 one after the other in a
#   thread: on the three-image set the pass took 339 us where AMD's takes 82, in a steady dispatch and in a reset one
#   alike; as it stands now it takes 85. Both the pass file and ffx_frameinterpolation.cpp decide by the same two values
#   of the dispatch.)
#
# MEASURED: see docs\reports\fg-3\own-build-2026-10-06\stage-3 (the table of cuts). On a frame where MANY pixels ask for
# inpainting the 16 pixels of a thread are inpainted one after the other: the same work, less spread out. The bench's
# picture asks at the edges of what moves, as a game's does; a frame that is all holes was not measured.
what the inpainting pass (I10), when no HUD-less image is attached and no debug drawing asked for, runs one thread for 4 by 4 pixels and calls AMD's function only for a pixel whose weight asks for inpainting; otherwise it runs as AMD's
passes ffx_frameinterpolation_inpainting_pass
wave64 it edits the pass's file, which both the cs_6_2 and the cs_6_6 (wave64) variants are compiled from, and the pass's dispatch in ffx_frameinterpolation.cpp, which runs on every device; no wave operation; not run here on a wave64 device
file framegeneration/fsr3/internal/shaders/ffx_frameinterpolation_inpainting_pass.hlsl
--- old
    computeInpainting(iPxPos);
--- new
    // (the mod's cut fi-inpainting-by-blocks) with a HUD-less image attached or a debug drawing asked for, AMD's function
    // may change any pixel: one thread a pixel, as AMD dispatches it (ffx_frameinterpolation.cpp decides by the same values)
    if (GetHUDLessAttachedFactor() == 1
        || (GetDispatchFlags() & (FFX_FRAMEINTERPOLATION_DISPATCH_DRAW_DEBUG_TEAR_LINES | FFX_FRAMEINTERPOLATION_DISPATCH_DRAW_DEBUG_RESET_INDICATORS)) != 0)
    {
        computeInpainting(iPxPos);
        return;
    }
    // otherwise this thread's 4 by 4 pixels, and AMD's function for a pixel whose weight asks for inpainting
    const FfxInt32x2 iCovered = ((DisplaySize() + 7) / 8) * 8;      // what AMD's dispatch covered: one group for 8 by 8 pixels
    for (FfxInt32 iBlockY = 0; iBlockY < 4; iBlockY++)
    {
        for (FfxInt32 iBlockX = 0; iBlockX < 4; iBlockX++)
        {
            const FfxInt32x2 iPx = iPxPos * 4 + FfxInt32x2(iBlockX, iBlockY);
            if (iPx.x < iCovered.x && iPx.y < iCovered.y)
            {
                if (rw_inpainting_mask[iPx] > FFX_FRAMEINTERPOLATION_EPSILON)
                {
                    computeInpainting(iPx);
                }
            }
        }
    }
--- end
file framegeneration/fsr3/internal/ffx_frameinterpolation.cpp
--- old
        scheduleDispatch(contextPrivate, &contextPrivate->pipelineInpainting, displayDispatchSizeX, displayDispatchSizeY);
--- new
        // (the mod's cut fi-inpainting-by-blocks: with no HUD-less image and no debug drawing a thread of this pass handles
        // 4 by 4 pixels; the pass file decides by the same two values)
        if (contextPrivate->constants.HUDLessAttachedFactor == 1
            || (params->flags & (FFX_FRAMEINTERPOLATION_DISPATCH_DRAW_DEBUG_TEAR_LINES | FFX_FRAMEINTERPOLATION_DISPATCH_DRAW_DEBUG_RESET_INDICATORS)) != 0)
        {
            scheduleDispatch(contextPrivate, &contextPrivate->pipelineInpainting, displayDispatchSizeX, displayDispatchSizeY);
        }
        else
        {
            scheduleDispatch(contextPrivate, &contextPrivate->pipelineInpainting, (displayDispatchSizeX + 3) / 4, (displayDispatchSizeY + 3) / 4);
        }
--- end
